//? Title: Signed radix sort: actual byte passes and stable origin identities
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A signed integer sort can exploit fixed-width digits rather than compare
arbitrary key pairs. The native LSD radix producer flips the sign bit, stably
scatters one byte at a time and restores signed values after eight passes. This
report reads actual histograms, prefix offsets and original input identities.
The original eight positive keys and signed-key example remain; the old decimal
report mirror is replaced by the library actual base256 passes.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

Two's-complement signed order becomes unsigned order under
\(u=\mathrm{uint64}(x)\mathbin\oplus2^{63}\). For byte r, count its occurrences
and prefix-sum the counts to obtain each bucket start. Scattering in input order
increments each bucket cursor, preserving order among equal current digits.
$$ \operatorname{stable\_sort}_{d_r}\circ\cdots\circ\operatorname{stable\_sort}_{d_0} $$
\alttext(Stable sorting successively more significant digits preserves the order already established by lower digits.)
Induction on processed bytes proves ordering by the low r+1 bytes. The eighth
pass therefore orders all biased words, and undoing the bias recovers ascending
signed keys. The producer moves original indices beside keys; equal final keys
retain increasing original indices. For n>=2, actual work includes16n digit
reads,8n scatter pairs and2048 prefix updates, excluding allocation and sign
conversion. Fixed-width cost is \(O(8(n+256))\), with linear scratch storage;
empty and singleton inputs skip all passes.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native concept diagram read the same producing execution. Every
block below is a complete editable C++ program.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/radix_sort_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/modular_exponentiation.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/integer_arithmetic.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <climits>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/number_theory.hpp>
#include <numeric>
#include <numbers>
#include <random>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <set>
#include <array>
#include <tuple>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/prufer.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/flow_diagram.hpp>
#include <datamunge/geometry/hull_algorithms.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/sampling_analysis.hpp>
#include <iomanip>
#include <bit>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/radix_sort.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    auto t = a::radix_sort_trace({256, 1, 257, 0});
    const auto &s = t.passes[0];
    std::print("\n| actual byte shift | actual pass values | actual original identities |\n| ---: | --- | " "--- |\n");
    for (const auto &e : t.passes)
        std::print("| {} | `{}` | `{}` |\n", e.shift, e.values, e.origins);

    g::Diagram d;
    d.node("n0", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual byte-zero counts\n0: {}, 1: {}", s.counts[0],
                       s.counts[1]));
    d.node("n1", {10, 0}, std::format("Actual first stable pass\n{}", s.values));
    d.node("n2", {20, 0},
           std::format("Actual complete signed order\n{}", t.values));
    d.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    d.save_svg("build/radix_sort_tiny.svg");
}
```
