> API and interpretation

The original decimal illustration is not labelled as native work: its input is
retained and shown through the canonical byte producer instead. Intermediate
signed decodings need not be sorted until the eighth pass; actual origin
identities prove stability at every pass. The original1000-trial signed/extreme
std::sort cross-check moves to native regression verification. Fixed eight-pass
linearity is specific to64-bit keys and radix256, not a contradiction of the
comparison-sort lower bound. History stores actual keys/origins plus bucket data
and is independently bounded.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

